To use ProxySQL in front of multiple MySQL regions, you deploy a local ProxySQL instance alongside each Joomla web server and use query mirroring (mirror_hostgroup) to asynchronously duplicate write traffic to your remote regions.
Running ProxySQL locally on each web server ensures Joomla gets fast local reads while ProxySQL handles the cross-region network overhead in the background.

Architecture Overview

• Hostgroup 10 (Local Primary): Your local region's MySQL server (handles all SELECT reads and primary INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE writes).
• Hostgroup 20 (Remote Region 2): Secondary region's MySQL server (receives mirrored writes).
• Hostgroup 30 (Remote Region 3): Tertiary region's MySQL server (receives mirrored writes).

Step 1: Prevent Primary Key Collisions

Before routing writes to multiple regions, configure auto_increment offsets on each MySQL server so regional writes never generate duplicate IDs for Joomla articles, users, or sessions.
• Region 1 MySQL (my.cnf):ini
auto_increment_increment = 3
auto_increment_offset = 1
Use code with caution.
• Region 2 MySQL (my.cnf):ini
auto_increment_increment = 3
auto_increment_offset = 2
Use code with caution.
• Region 3 MySQL (my.cnf):ini
auto_increment_increment = 3
auto_increment_offset = 3
Use code with caution.
(Set auto_increment_increment to match your total number of active regions.)

Step 2: Configure ProxySQL Backends & Users

Connect to the ProxySQL admin interface (mysql -u admin -padmin -h 127.0.0.1 -P 6032) on your Region 1 server and register your databases and Joomla database user:
sql
-- 1. Add your MySQL servers to their respective hostgroups
INSERT INTO mysql_servers (hostgroup_id, hostname, port) VALUES 
(10, '10.0.1.10', 3306), -- Local Region 1 MySQL
(20, '10.0.2.10', 3306), -- Remote Region 2 MySQL
(30, '10.0.3.10', 3306); -- Remote Region 3 MySQL

-- 2. Add your Joomla database user (must exist on all MySQL servers)
INSERT INTO mysql_users (username, password, default_hostgroup) 
VALUES ('joomla_user', 'your_strong_password', 10);

-- 3. Apply changes to runtime and save to disk
LOAD MYSQL SERVERS TO RUNTIME;
SAVE MYSQL SERVERS TO DISK;
LOAD MYSQL USERS TO RUNTIME;
SAVE MYSQL USERS TO DISK;
Use code with caution.

Step 3: Set Up Write Mirroring Rules

ProxySQL's mirror_hostgroup feature executes a query on the primary hostgroup (destination_hostgroup) and asynchronously fires a copy to the mirror hostgroup without making Joomla wait for the remote region to respond.
Note: A single rule only accepts one mirror_hostgroup. To mirror to two remote regions, chain a second rule or point mirroring to a proxy/cascading setup. For two regions total (Local + 1 Remote), run:
sql
-- Rule 1: Send all writes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, REPLACE) to Local (10) and mirror to Region 2 (20)
INSERT INTO mysql_query_rules (rule_id, active, match_pattern, destination_hostgroup, mirror_hostgroup, apply)
VALUES (10, 1, '^(INSERT|UPDATE|DELETE|REPLACE)', 10, 20, 1);

-- Rule 2: Send all SELECT reads strictly to Local (10) for low latency
INSERT INTO mysql_query_rules (rule_id, active, match_pattern, destination_hostgroup, apply)
VALUES (20, 1, '^SELECT', 10, 1);

-- Apply and save rules
LOAD MYSQL QUERY RULES TO RUNTIME;
SAVE MYSQL QUERY RULES TO DISK;
Use code with caution.
(Note: Exclude Joomla's #__session table from mirroring by adding a higher-priority rule—e.g., rule_id = 5 matching jos_session with no mirror_hostgroup—so temporary user sessions don't flood your cross-region bandwidth.)

Step 4: Point Joomla to ProxySQL

Update your Joomla configuration.php file so Joomla talks to ProxySQL instead of connecting directly to MySQL:
php
public $host = '127.0.0.1:6033'; // ProxySQL default SQL traffic port
public $user = 'joomla_user';
public $password = 'your_strong_password';
public $db = 'joomla_db';
Use code with caution.

Important Caveats for Production

• Fire-and-forget mirroring: ProxySQL's mirror_hostgroup is asynchronous and best-effort. If Region 2 is temporarily unreachable over the WAN, ProxySQL drops the mirrored query rather than queuing it indefinitely—so a network blip will cause data drift.
• Connection encryption: Always enable SSL/TLS (use_ssl=1 in mysql_servers) when mirroring unencrypted SQL queries across public internet regions.
• Non-deterministic queries: Queries using NOW(), RAND(), or UUID() will evaluate separately on each server, which can cause slight timestamp differences across regions.
